#
#  CDC spun off their disk-drive business as Imprimis, which was later bought 
#  by Seagate, so direct all questions about CDC and Imprimis drives to Seagate.
#
#  Fujitsu lists some format parameters for their drives on the WWW, at
#  http://www.fujitsu-europe.com/t_sun01.htm
#
#  Quantum lists some format parameters for their drives on the WWW, at
#  http://support.quantum.com/appnotes/sun.htm
#
# TIPS for rolling your own format.dat
#
# In SunOS 5.3 (aka Solaris 2.3) and later, the format program supports the 
# automatic querying of SCSI drives for their parameters, and a format.dat 
# entry is not strictly necessary.
#
# For SCSI disks, any combination of cylinders, heads and sectors that does 
# not add up to more than the rated formatted capacity of the drive 
# will usually work, because the SCSI specification hides most of the drive
# details from the host. 
#
# A program which queries a SCSI drive for its parameters and generates a 
# format.dat entry for it is scsiinfo, which is available for anonymous 
# ftp from ftp.cdf.toronto.edu (128.100.31.2):/pub/scsiinfo/scsiinfo-*.shar.
# [ Yes, it's an attempt to put myself out of the format faq business. :-) ]
# Moreover, SCSI drives may be queried at great length using the scsiping 
# program, which is freely available for anonymous ftp from 
# ftp.cdf.toronto.edu (128.100.31.2) and ftp.fwi.uva.nl (146.50.4.20) in 
# /pub/scsiping/scsiping-2.0.shar. 
#
# woods@most.weird.com (Greg A. Woods) makes the following suggestions
# for finding geometry information if it's not here, and if you can't run
# scsiping or scsiinfo:
#
#     1. run format and choose the "Other type" disk
#     
#     2. supply reasonable values for the disk geometry
#     
#     3. go back to the main menu and enter the defect mananagement menu and
#     load the "original manufacturer's defect list", then commit the list
#     
#     3a. optionally print the defect list to get clues on the geometry and go
#     back to step 1.
#     
#     4. enter the "analysis" menu and run the "setup" option to turn *on* the
#     "extended messages" (and optionally set other values, such as number of
#     passes to one)
#     
#     5. go back to the main menu and format the drive
#     
#     If you didn't get all the geometry right, the extended messages printed
#     will show the correct values (according to the drive) in parenthesis
#     after the values you did enter.  Run format again and enter the correct
#     geometry, or write a new /etc/format.dat entry.
#     
# For SunOS 4.1.2 and earlier, replace all occurrences of "SCSI" by "MD21".
# For SunOS 5.x, replace partition names a, b, c, etc. with 0, 1, 2, etc. 
# For Solbourne systems, replace all occurrences of "SCSI" by "IOASIC".
#
# When partitioning, do not make a partition larger than 2GB (2097152 KB, or
# 4194304 blocks), since SunOS does not support partitions larger than 2GB. 

#
# This is the search path for 'format'. It contains all the disks that
# will be searched for if no disk list is given on the command line.
#

search_path =   xd0, xd1, xd2, xd3, xd4, xd5, xd6, xd7, xd8, xd9, xd10, xd11, \
                xd12, xd13, xd14, xd15, xy[0-3], sd[0-7], sd8, sd9, sd10, \
                sd11, sd12, sd13, sd14, sd15, id0[0-3][0-7]

